DUAL-LICENSE NOTICE — read this preamble before § 1 (Proprietary terms)
================================================================================

The EFE repository ships under two licenses, applied per-directory :

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Path                                    │ License                         │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ /clients/python/**                      │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /clients/typescript/**                  │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /clients/java/**                        │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /clients/go/**                          │ Apache License 2.0              │
  │ /crates/efe-nano-sdk/**                 │ MIT (see ./LICENSE-MIT)         │
  │ Everything else (server, ops, models)   │ EFE Proprietary (see § 1 below) │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

The `/crates/efe-nano-sdk/**` row was added on 2026-09-05 and grants nothing new :
decision D-M50-CRATES-001 (ACCEPTED, 2026-05-18) already reads « SDK MIT-licensed,
runtime commercial », and the crate's own `Cargo.toml` has declared `license = "MIT"`
since M50. What was missing was the MIT text itself — `crates/efe-nano-sdk/README.md`
pointed at a workspace `LICENSE-MIT` that did not exist. This file was the outlier,
not the manifest. The runtime crate `efe-nano` remains proprietary, which is the whole
point of the split.

⚠ **The four `/clients/<lang>/**` trees and `/protos/` left this repository on
2026-09-05** (step D2 of the roadmap), together with 38 other directories that
belong to the EFE research project rather than to the Scella product. They were
**moved, not deleted** : `Downloads/vrai/EFE-arbre-2026-09-05/`, verified file by
file (SHA-256 for every regular file, target for every symlink).

The rows above are **kept, not removed**, and that is deliberate : an Apache-2.0
grant already made on a published artifact (PyPI wheel, npm package, Maven jar, Go
module) is not withdrawn by moving its source out of a repository. What changed is
where the source lives, not what was granted. Each of those SDK directories carried
its own `LICENSE` (full Apache-2.0 text) and `NOTICE` (the attribution required by
Apache-2.0 § 4(d)) ; both travelled with it.

What this repository ships today is proprietary (§ 1), except
`crates/efe-nano-sdk/**`, which is MIT — see `./LICENSE-MIT`.

The carve-out is intentional and reflects EFE's commercial model :
client SDKs are open-source so customers can audit, vendor, and fork
them ; the server, model bundles, operator tooling, helm charts and
proto definitions are proprietary EFE intellectual property.

Boundary rule : a file is open-source **only** if one of these holds ; everything
else is proprietary per § 1.

  (a) it lives under `/clients/<lang>/` AND that SDK declares Apache-2.0 where its
      ecosystem declares licenses — `pyproject.toml`, `package.json` or `pom.xml`
      for Python, TypeScript and Java ; the **`LICENSE` file of the module** for Go ;

  (b) it lives under a path listed in the table above with a license other than
      « EFE Proprietary », and the corresponding license text ships at the repository
      root (`./LICENSE-MIT` for `/crates/efe-nano-sdk/**`).

Clause (a) was corrected on 2026-09-05. It previously named `go.mod` as the place
where the Go SDK declares Apache-2.0 — **`go.mod` has no license field**, and none
can exist : the Go toolchain has no such mechanism. Read literally, the old rule made
the Go SDK proprietary while its directory shipped an Apache-2.0 `LICENSE` and a
`NOTICE`. A boundary rule that no artifact can satisfy is not a boundary.

Proto definitions under `/protos/efe/` were dual-licensed by reference : when
consumed from a `/clients/<lang>/` build (the SDK proto codegen), the generated
stubs inherit Apache-2.0 ; when consumed from the server build, they remain
proprietary. The `.proto` source files themselves are proprietary — they cannot be
forked and redistributed outside the SDK-codegen path without an explicit license
from EFE. **This clause governs artifacts already generated ; `/protos/` is no
longer in this repository** (see the notice above).

For dual-license questions : legal@efe.ai

